TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design (Launch, Orbit, Ground Control, EOL)"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction / Executive Summary"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design - Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design - Orbit (COE table)"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design - Orbit / EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications (STK access report)"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Orbit (STK + J2 inclination plot)"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Screenshots included; no evidence of attached STK software files"},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"Report structure (Exec Summary, Introduction, Mission Design, Conclusion, Bibliography)"},{"id":"R14","met":"partial","where":"Figures/tables have captions but no figure/table numbers"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography: Wikipedia cited as [6]; references are bare URLs"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit","issue":"\"A very high altitude would yield low resolution images which is also desirable\" – should read 'undesirable'; contradicts design logic."},{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit","issue":"\"significant J2 perturbations as a result of its highly polar inclination\" is imprecise: RAAN precession ∝ cos(i) is zero at i=90°; SSO relies on retrograde inclination (~98°), not polar proximity per se."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Graveyard-orbit discussion is largely irrelevant for a LEO satellite; EOL paragraph is redundant/contradictory (\"without thrusters... uncontrolled descent... However, the ZYZZsat is without thrust capability\")."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Drag-sail sentence is incomplete: \"utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable\"."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Wikipedia used as a source, explicitly disallowed; references lack titles, authors and access dates."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Access table numbering is inconsistent (first row unnumbered, duplicate access 14/15) – possibly an OCR artefact but should be checked."}],"overall":"A well-structured, technically sound preliminary mission design addressing all space-segment requirements with genuine STK evidence and hand calculations. Weakened by missing software files, unnumbered figures/tables, bare-URL references including Wikipedia, and several technical/editorial slips. Weighted score ≈ 7.3/10.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"All four requirements addressed: primary mission (fluid-lensing reef imaging), rationale (bleaching threat with cited statistics), single-spacecraft justification, and users/funders. Well referenced. Slightly limited depth on user/customer distinction prevents a top mark.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM)"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":48.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"All major requirements covered: launch site/vehicle with rationale, SSO with full COE table, manoeuvres (none until EOL), ground station selection logic, STK access report quantifying contact frequency/duration, 5-year duration and drag-sail EOL. STK screenshots and inclination-vs-altitude hand calculation included. Minor technical slips and no software files attached keep it from 9-10.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Design - Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit","quote":"an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The most promising alternative is a ‘drag sail’"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions are specific and trace back to evidence, including honest caveats (Vega E availability, alternatives). Somewhat brief and omits EOL/communications summary detail, so at the lower end of Accomplished.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear structure with headings and executive summary, but figures/tables lack numbered captions (e.g. 'Figure 1'), bibliography is bare URLs without titles/authors, Wikipedia is cited (explicitly disallowed), and several typos/incomplete sentences ('a Earth observation', 'current owned', broken drag-sail sentence) persist.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"STK 3D view of orbit: 12 hours apart"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable for low-earth orbit altitudes"}]}]}
